1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
Four thousand seven hundred seventeen
dollars.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,400
That's the penalty on one missed
withdrawal from a five hundred thousand
dollar

3
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:08,450
I R A…

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:12,830
the first year the government says you
have to take money out, whether you need

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:13,880
it or not.

6
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,760
Miss the date, and a quarter of what you
should have taken goes to the I R S.

7
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:20,340
This is The Fine Print.

8
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:25,750
By the end you'll know three lines of the
rule: when the clock starts, how the

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:30,510
number is figured, and the one-time choice
in the first year that quietly doubles

10
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:31,560
your tax bill.

11
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:34,000
This is education, not advice.

12
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:37,840
The account in the example is an example,
not a real person.

13
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:39,870
Line one.

14
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:41,020
When the clock starts.

15
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:43,580
A traditional I R A,

16
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:48,140
a four oh one k, a four oh three b: money
that went in before tax.

17
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:54,350
The I R S lets it grow untaxed for
decades, and then at seventy-three, it
starts

18
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:55,460
calling the money out.

19
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,780
The required minimum distribution.

20
00:00:58,260 --> 00:00:59,700
Call it the R M D.

21
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:04,370
If you were born in nineteen sixty or
later, the law already moved your start

22
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:05,420
to seventy-five.

23
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:08,480
Everyone born before that: seventy-three.

24
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:11,680
Roth I R As are exempt while you're alive.

25
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:13,360
Since twenty twenty-four,

26
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,100
Roth four oh one k money is exempt too.

27
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:19,300
The clock runs on the pre-tax accounts
only.

28
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:21,370
Line two.

29
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:22,640
How the number is figured.

30
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:26,530
Take the account balance on December
thirty-first of last year.

31
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:30,060
Divide it by a number the I R S publishes
for your age.

32
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:33,910
At seventy-three, that number is
twenty-six point five.

33
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:38,060
Five hundred thousand dollars, divided by
twenty-six point five.

34
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:40,840
Eighteen thousand eight hundred
sixty-eight dollars.

35
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:42,680
That's the floor.

36
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:44,240
You can take more.

37
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:45,680
You can't take less.

38
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:51,310
And every dollar of it is ordinary income,
on top of Social Security, on top of

39
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:53,540
the pension, in the year it comes out.

40
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,260
The divisor shrinks every year.

41
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:59,260
At seventy-five it's twenty-four point
six.

42
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:01,660
At eighty, twenty point two.

43
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:04,100
At ninety, twelve point two.

44
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:06,830
On the same five hundred thousand, that's

45
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,880
twenty thousand three hundred at
seventy-five,

46
00:02:08,930 --> 00:02:13,880
twenty-four thousand seven hundred at
eighty, and forty-one thousand at ninety.

47
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:17,080
The account doesn't have to grow for the
withdrawal to.

48
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:20,920
Now the penalty.

49
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:25,980
Miss the full amount by December
thirty-first, and the excise tax is
twenty-five

50
00:02:26,030 --> 00:02:27,660
percent of what you didn't take.

51
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:32,310
On our eighteen thousand eight hundred
sixty-eight: four thousand seven hundred

52
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,360
seventeen dollars.

53
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:38,610
Fix it within two years, take the money
and file the form, and the rate drops

54
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:39,870
to ten percent.

55
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:41,420
Eighteen hundred eighty-seven.

56
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:46,830
The form is fifty-three twenty-nine, and
it has a line for asking the I R S to

57
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,160
waive the penalty if you have a reasonable
cause and you've corrected it.

58
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:52,720
People do get it waived.

59
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:55,300
Nobody gets it waived without filing.

60
00:02:56,640 --> 00:02:58,320
Here's the part that catches people.

61
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Line three.

62
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:04,100
The first year has two deadlines, and the
second one is a trap.

63
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:07,960
The first R M D isn't due December
thirty-first.

64
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:10,440
It's due April first of the following
year.

65
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,100
That sounds like a gift.

66
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:13,640
Four extra months.

67
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:19,480
But the second R M D, for the next year,
is still due that December thirty-first.

68
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:23,520
Wait until April, and two withdrawals land
in the same tax year.

69
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,140
Run the number.

70
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:28,540
Turn seventy-three this year with five
hundred thousand in the I R A.

71
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:32,560
The first withdrawal is eighteen thousand
eight hundred sixty-eight.

72
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,360
Take it by December, and it sits alone on
this year's return.

73
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:38,040
Wait until April.

74
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:39,880
Now it lands next year.

75
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:44,720
And next year's own withdrawal, say the
account grew to five hundred twenty-five

76
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:47,180
thousand, divided by twenty-five point
five…

77
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:49,420
twenty thousand five hundred eighty-eight.

78
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:51,600
Both in one year.

79
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:56,090
Thirty-nine thousand four hundred
fifty-six dollars of income, where there
should

80
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:57,140
have been eighteen.

81
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:01,590
Say it's a couple, and their other income
already uses most of the twelve percent

82
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:02,630
bracket.

83
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:06,580
Twenty-four thousand of room left before
the twenty-two percent bracket for twenty

84
00:04:06,630 --> 00:04:07,630
twenty-seven.

85
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:11,080
Taken one per year, both withdrawals fit
under the line.

86
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:12,750
Twelve percent on each.

87
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:16,340
Forty-seven hundred dollars of tax, across
the two years.

88
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:19,620
Doubled up, the second one spills over the
line.

89
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:22,640
Twenty-two percent on fifteen thousand of
it.

90
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:24,880
Sixty-two hundred seventy-five dollars.

91
00:04:24,930 --> 00:04:29,580
Fifteen hundred dollars more, for taking
the same money four months later.

92
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:33,440
And that's before the other line that
reads your return two years later.

93
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:37,720
The Medicare surcharge we ran on Tuesday
looks at this exact number.

94
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:40,690
A doubled year can be the year that
crosses it.

95
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,880
Two more lines that move the number.

96
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:44,250
One.

97
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:48,410
If you don't need the money and you give
to charity anyway, a qualified charitable

98
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:51,880
distribution sends it from the I R A
straight to the charity.

99
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,620
It counts toward the R M D, and it never
shows up as income.

100
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:00,270
Up to one hundred eleven thousand dollars
for twenty twenty-six, and you can start

101
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:03,080
at seventy and a half, before the R M D
clock does.

102
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:04,290
Two.

103
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:08,670
You can take the R M D from any one I R A
to cover all your I R As.

104
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:11,380
But a four oh one k has to pay its own.

105
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:14,700
Two old employer plans, two separate
withdrawals.

106
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:17,700
The penalty is per account that came up
short.

107
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:19,340
How to check yours.

108
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:22,980
Pull the December thirty-first statement
for every pre-tax account.

109
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:24,420
Add them up.

110
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:29,070
Find your age on the I R S Uniform
Lifetime Table, it's one page in
Publication

111
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:30,300
five ninety B.

112
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:31,290
Divide.

113
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:35,450
That's the floor for this year, and the
custodian's letter in January should say

114
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:36,500
the same number.

115
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:41,190
Then look at the calendar: if this is your
first year, decide now whether the

116
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:43,440
first one lands in December or April.

117
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,470
A fee-only fiduciary or a C P A can run
the two-year version with your bracket

118
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:49,310
in it.

119
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:50,360
Here's the pattern.

120
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:52,480
The money was always going to be taxed.

121
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:56,160
The rule decides when, and the calendar
decides how much.

122
00:05:56,760 --> 00:05:59,960
Take the floor on time and it's an
ordinary line on the return.

123
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:02,560
Miss it and a quarter goes to the penalty.

124
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:05,950
Stack two in one year and the bracket
takes its cut.

125
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,340
Same account, same money, three different
bills.

126
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:11,260
One thing this week.

127
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:15,510
If you're seventy-three or older, find the
number on this year's custodian letter

128
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:17,120
and check that it's already out.

129
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:21,990
If you turn seventy-three next year, write
April first and December thirty-first

130
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:23,840
on the same page, and pick one.

131
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,260
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

132
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:28,740
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

133
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:32,060
Will your money last as long as you do?

